  "step": [
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1,
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "So, you've got a foreclosed property that's looking a little worse for wear and definitely needs a fresh coat of paint. Where do you even begin? First things first, you've really got to inspect the current state of the walls. Look for peeling paint, any suspicious stains, signs of moisture, or cracks. Foreclosed homes can have hidden damage from being vacant, so don't skip this step! Once you've got a good idea of what you're dealing with, you can start to define the scope, room by room. Think about protecting everything – floors, fixtures, anything you're keeping in the space. Then comes the real work: patching, priming, and finally, painting. Make sure you do a thorough walkthrough afterward for any touch-ups. It's easy to underestimate the prep work, but it's super important for a good finish. If you're dealing with extensive damage or a property built before 1978, lead-safe handling might be necessary. Getting a professional involved for complex situations like these can save you a ton of headaches and ensure everything's done right. Give HH Remodeling LLC a call for expert advice and a clear plan."
    }
  ],
